===============================================================================
pktgen-dpdk with ENETC4 VF on NXP i.MX95 EVK (DPDK 25.11 / pktgen 26.03)
===============================================================================

Overview
========
This guide covers cross-compiling pktgen-dpdk (traffic generator) for the NXP
i.MX95 EVK target using the ENETC4 Virtual Function (VF) DPDK PMD driver with
the uio_pci_generic kernel driver (no IOMMU / VFIO required).

Two build approaches are described:

  A - Shared DPDK  : pktgen + DPDK .so files + system libs on target
  B - Static DPDK  : DPDK .a archives baked into pktgen binary (recommended)
                     Only system shared libs (libc, libpcap) needed on target.

A fully static binary (no .so at all) is not possible because static libc.a
and libpcap.a are not typically available in the Arm GNU sysroot.


Prerequisites - Host machine
=============================

1. Arm GNU cross-toolchain (aarch64-none-linux-gnu)
   Download from: https://developer.arm.com/downloads/-/arm-gnu-toolchain-downloads
   Tested version: arm-gnu-toolchain-13.2.Rel1-x86_64-aarch64-none-linux-gnu

   Install to any directory, e.g. /opt/toolchain/arm-gnu-13.2/
   Add the bin/ subdirectory to PATH:

     export TOOLCHAIN=/opt/toolchain/arm-gnu-13.2/bin
     export PATH=$TOOLCHAIN:$PATH

   The following tools from this toolchain are required:
     aarch64-none-linux-gnu-gcc
     aarch64-none-linux-gnu-g++
     aarch64-none-linux-gnu-ar
     aarch64-none-linux-gnu-strip
     aarch64-linux-gnu-pkg-config   (usually from host distro libtool package)

2. Meson >= 0.58 and ninja
     pip3 install --user meson ninja
   or
     apt-get install meson ninja-build

3. Python 3.6+, git

4. Aarch64 sysroot containing:
     libnuma headers and shared library  (libnuma-dev)
     libpcap headers and shared library  (libpcap-dev)
     libbsd headers and shared library   (libbsd-dev)

   The sysroot can be obtained from:
   - NXP i.MX Yocto SDK:  bitbake meta-toolchain (or -populate-sdk)
   - NXP Linarox / IMX Linux BSP SDK installer (produces a sysroot directory)
   - Manually assembled by multiarch-apt or debootstrap for aarch64

   Set the path:
     export SYSROOT=/path/to/aarch64-sysroot

   Verify the key files exist:
     $SYSROOT/usr/include/pcap.h
     $SYSROOT/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libpcap.so
     $SYSROOT/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libnuma.so


Cross-Compilation File for pktgen
==================================
pktgen-dpdk does not use the DPDK config/arm/ cross files directly.  A
separate meson cross-compilation INI file is needed for pktgen.  Create it
once and keep it alongside the pktgen source tree (or in any convenient
location):

  File: cross_arm64_imx.ini

  [binaries]
  c   = '/opt/toolchain/arm-gnu-13.2/bin/aarch64-none-linux-gnu-gcc'
  cpp = '/opt/toolchain/arm-gnu-13.2/bin/aarch64-none-linux-gnu-g++'
  ar  = '/opt/toolchain/arm-gnu-13.2/bin/aarch64-none-linux-gnu-ar'
  as  = '/opt/toolchain/arm-gnu-13.2/bin/aarch64-none-linux-gnu-as'
  strip = '/opt/toolchain/arm-gnu-13.2/bin/aarch64-none-linux-gnu-strip'
  pkgconfig  = 'aarch64-linux-gnu-pkg-config'
  pkg-config = 'aarch64-linux-gnu-pkg-config'
  pcap-config = ''

  [host_machine]
  system     = 'linux'
  cpu_family = 'aarch64'
  cpu        = 'armv8.2-a'
  endian     = 'little'

NOTE: Adjust the toolchain path to match your installation.

Cross-Compilation File for DPDK
================================
DPDK ships its own cross files under config/arm/.  The file that matches the
Arm GNU aarch64-none-linux-gnu toolchain for i.MX platforms is:

  config/arm/arm64_imx_linux_gnu_gcc

This file already references aarch64-none-linux-gnu-gcc and sets
platform=imx.  Use it directly when building DPDK - no changes to DPDK source
code are required:

  meson setup arm64-build \
    --cross-file config/arm/arm64_imx_linux_gnu_gcc \
    ...


Step 1 - Clone Sources
=======================

1a. Clone this DPDK repository (already done if you are reading this file):

  git clone <dpdk-repo-url> dpdk
  cd dpdk
  # This guide is tested against DPDK 25.11.x NXP branch.
  # Use the branch/tag recommended by your BSP release notes.

1b. Clone pktgen-dpdk:

  git clone https://github.com/pktgen/pktgen-dpdk.git pktgen-dpdk
  cd pktgen-dpdk
  git checkout pktgen-26.03.0

  Verify the version tag:
    git describe --tags
    # Expected: pktgen-26.03.0


Step 2 - Build DPDK Statically (Approach B, recommended)
==========================================================
The static DPDK build tree is consumed directly by pktgen via the
meson-uninstalled pkgconfig file.  No 'ninja install' step is needed.

  export SYSROOT=/path/to/aarch64-sysroot
  cd dpdk

  # Remove any previous arm64 build directory
  rm -rf arm64-build

  meson setup arm64-build \
    --cross-file config/arm/arm64_imx_linux_gnu_gcc \
    --default-library=static \
    -Denable_kmods=false \
    -Dtests=false \
    -Dc_args="-I${SYSROOT}/usr/include" \
    -Dc_link_args="-L${SYSROOT}/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu \
                   -L${SYSROOT}/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu"

  ninja -C arm64-build

After a successful build:
  arm64-build/lib/librte_*.a       (static archives)
  arm64-build/drivers/librte_*.a
  arm64-build/meson-uninstalled/   (pkgconfig used by pktgen)

Key meson options:

  --default-library=static   Produce .a archives instead of .so shared libs
  -Denable_kmods=false       Skip kernel module build
  -Dtests=false              Skip unit tests to save build time


Step 3 - Build pktgen with DPDK Statically Linked (Approach B)
================================================================
pktgen reads DPDK build info via pkg-config.  Point PKG_CONFIG_PATH at the
DPDK meson-uninstalled tree and the sysroot pkgconfig directory:

  export SYSROOT=/path/to/aarch64-sysroot
  export PKG_CONFIG_PATH=<path-to-dpdk>/arm64-build/meson-uninstalled:${SYSROOT}/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/pkgconfig

  cd pktgen-dpdk
  rm -rf arm64-build

  # -Dprefer_static=true causes meson to pass --static to pkg-config,
  # which activates the Libs.private whole-archive entries in the DPDK
  # .pc file so that .a archives are linked instead of .so files.
  meson setup arm64-build \
    --cross-file cross_arm64_imx.ini \
    --default-library=static \
    -Dprefer_static=true \
    -Dwerror=false \
    -Dbuildtype=release

  ninja -C arm64-build

Result: arm64-build/app/pktgen

Verify the binary:
  file arm64-build/app/pktgen
  # ELF 64-bit LSB executable, ARM aarch64 ...

  # Confirm no DPDK .so dependencies remain
  aarch64-none-linux-gnu-readelf -d arm64-build/app/pktgen | grep NEEDED
  # Expected output: libm.so.6  libpcap.so.0.8  libc.so.6
  # (no librte_*.so should appear)

Optional - strip debug symbols to reduce binary size:
  aarch64-none-linux-gnu-strip arm64-build/app/pktgen


Step 4 - Build DPDK as Shared Libraries (Approach A)
=====================================================
Use this if you prefer the classic shared-library deployment model.

  export SYSROOT=/path/to/aarch64-sysroot
  cd dpdk
  rm -rf arm64-build-shared

  meson setup arm64-build-shared \
    --cross-file config/arm/arm64_imx_linux_gnu_gcc \
    --default-library=shared \
    -Denable_kmods=false \
    -Dtests=false \
    -Dprefix=<path-to-dpdk>/arm64-build-shared/install \
    -Dc_args="-I${SYSROOT}/usr/include" \
    -Dc_link_args="-L${SYSROOT}/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu \
                   -L${SYSROOT}/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu"

  ninja -C arm64-build-shared
  ninja -C arm64-build-shared install

Build pktgen against shared DPDK:

  export PKG_CONFIG_PATH=<path-to-dpdk>/arm64-build-shared/install/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/pkgconfig:$PKG_CONFIG_PATH

  cd pktgen-dpdk
  rm -rf arm64-build-shared

  meson setup arm64-build-shared \
    --cross-file cross_arm64_imx.ini \
    -Dwerror=false \
    -Dbuildtype=release

  ninja -C arm64-build-shared

Result: arm64-build-shared/app/pktgen


libpcap - Where to Obtain It
==============================
libpcap is required at both build time (headers + .so for linking) and
runtime (shared library on the target).

Static build (Approach B):
  libpcap must be present in the sysroot as a shared library for linking
  during the pktgen build.  The resulting pktgen binary will have
  libpcap.so.0.8 as a runtime dependency and that library must exist on
  the target board.

Sources for aarch64 libpcap:
  - NXP i.MX Yocto SDK / BSP sysroot (recommended - matches target rootfs)
  - Debian/Ubuntu multiarch:
      dpkg --add-architecture arm64
      apt-get install libpcap-dev:arm64
      # Headers in /usr/include, .so in /usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/
  - Build from source (tcpdump.org):
      ./configure --host=aarch64-none-linux-gnu CC=aarch64-none-linux-gnu-gcc
      make

Runtime library on target:
  The libpcap .so is usually already present in standard i.MX BSP images.
  If not, copy from the sysroot:

    scp ${SYSROOT}/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libpcap.so.1.9.1 root@<board>:/usr/lib/
    ssh root@<board> "cd /usr/lib && ln -sf libpcap.so.1.9.1 libpcap.so.0.8 && ldconfig"


Step 5 - Deploy to Target Board
=================================

Approach B (Static DPDK - recommended):
  Only the pktgen binary and system runtime libraries need to be copied.

    TARGET=root@<board-ip>
    PKTGEN_BUILD=<path-to-pktgen-dpdk>/arm64-build
    SYSROOT=/path/to/aarch64-sysroot

    # Copy pktgen binary (DPDK baked in)
    scp ${PKTGEN_BUILD}/app/pktgen ${TARGET}:/usr/local/bin/
    ssh ${TARGET} chmod +x /usr/local/bin/pktgen

    # Copy system runtime libs if not already present on target
    scp ${SYSROOT}/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libpcap.so.1*  ${TARGET}:/usr/lib/
    scp ${SYSROOT}/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libnuma.so.1*  ${TARGET}:/usr/lib/
    scp ${SYSROOT}/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libbsd.so.0*   ${TARGET}:/usr/lib/
    ssh ${TARGET} ldconfig

Approach A (Shared DPDK):
  The pktgen binary, DPDK shared libraries, and system libraries must all
  be copied to the target.

    TARGET=root@<board-ip>
    DPDK_INST=<path-to-dpdk>/arm64-build-shared/install
    PKTGEN_BUILD=<path-to-pktgen-dpdk>/arm64-build-shared
    SYSROOT=/path/to/aarch64-sysroot

    scp ${PKTGEN_BUILD}/app/pktgen ${TARGET}:/usr/local/bin/

    # DPDK core shared libs
    scp ${DPDK_INST}/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/librte_*.so.26.0 ${TARGET}:/usr/lib/

    # DPDK PMD plugins
    ssh ${TARGET} mkdir -p /usr/lib/dpdk
    scp ${DPDK_INST}/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/dpdk/pmds-26.0/librte_*.so.26.0 \
        ${TARGET}:/usr/lib/dpdk/

    # System runtime libs
    scp ${SYSROOT}/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libpcap.so.1*  ${TARGET}:/usr/lib/
    scp ${SYSROOT}/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libnuma.so.1*  ${TARGET}:/usr/lib/
    scp ${SYSROOT}/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libbsd.so.0*   ${TARGET}:/usr/lib/

    ssh ${TARGET} "echo /usr/lib/dpdk > /etc/ld.so.conf.d/dpdk.conf && ldconfig"


Step 6 - Target Board Setup
=============================

6.1 Hugepages
-------------
pktgen requires hugepages.  Configure at least 512 x 2MB hugepages:

  # Mount hugetlbfs if not already mounted
  mkdir -p /mnt/huge
  mount -t hugetlbfs nodev /mnt/huge

  # Allocate 2MB hugepages (512 pages = 1 GB total)
  echo 512 > /sys/kernel/mm/hugepages/hugepages-2048kB/nr_hugepages

  # Verify
  cat /proc/meminfo | grep -i huge

For persistent allocation add to /etc/default/grub:
  GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX="... default_hugepagesz=2M hugepagesz=2M hugepages=512"
And run update-grub.

6.2 ENETC4 VF - uio_pci_generic binding
-----------------------------------------
The ENETC4 DPDK PMD uses uio_pci_generic for DMA access (no IOMMU required).

On i.MX95 EVK the PF is at PCIe address 0002:00:00.0 and VFs appear at
0002:00:10.x and above after SR-IOV is enabled.  The exact VF address
depends on the number of VFs created and the PCI topology.

  # Load kernel modules
  modprobe uio
  modprobe uio_pci_generic

  # Create 1 VF on the enetc4 PF (adjust PF address if different)
  echo 1 > /sys/bus/pci/devices/0002:00:10.0/sriov_numvfs

  # Verify VF appeared
  lspci | grep -i enetc
  # Expected: 0002:00:12.0 Ethernet controller: NXP ...

  # Grant the VF trust mode on the PF netdev (required for promiscuous mode)
  ip link set eth1 vf 0 trust on

  # Unbind VF from its default Linux driver
  echo 0002:00:12.0 > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/fsl_enetc_vf/unbind

  # Bind VF to uio_pci_generic
  echo uio_pci_generic > /sys/bus/pci/devices/0002:00:12.0/driver_override
  echo 0002:00:12.0 > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/uio_pci_generic/bind

  # Verify binding
  ls /sys/bus/pci/drivers/uio_pci_generic/
  # 0002:00:12.0 should appear in the list

  # Verify /dev/uioX node appeared
  ls /dev/uio*

Notes:
- The PF net interface name (eth1 above) may differ on your board.
  Use 'ip link' or 'ls /sys/class/net/' to identify it.
- If the VF is already unbound (no driver), skip the unbind step.
- To make the binding persistent across reboots, add the modprobe and
  bind commands to /etc/rc.local or create a udev rule.

6.3 CPU Isolation (optional but recommended for accurate measurements)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Add to /etc/default/grub and run update-grub:
  GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX="... isolcpus=2-5 nohz_full=2-5 rcu_nocbs=2-5"


Step 7 - Running pktgen
========================

7.1 Basic command syntax
  pktgen [EAL options] -- [pktgen options]

  Use -a (--allow) to whitelist the VF PCI address.
  Map lcores to ports with -m "lcore.port".

7.2 Single VF port (1 TX/RX queue)

  pktgen \
    -l 0,2 \
    -n 4 \
    --proc-type primary \
    --file-prefix pktgen \
    -a 0002:00:12.0 \
    -- \
    -T \
    -P \
    -m "2.0"

  Option summary:
    -l 0,2          Use lcore 0 (main) and lcore 2 (port 0 TX/RX)
    -n 4            4 memory channels
    -a 0002:00:12.0 Allow/whitelist VF PCI address
    -T              Enable color terminal output
    -P              Enable promiscuous mode on all ports
    -m "2.0"        Map lcore 2 to port 0, queue 0

7.3 Two VF ports back-to-back throughput test

  # First enable 2 VFs:
  echo 2 > /sys/bus/pci/devices/0002:00:10.0/sriov_numvfs
  ip link set eth1 vf 0 trust on
  ip link set eth1 vf 1 trust on
  # Bind both VFs to uio_pci_generic (0002:00:12.0 and 0002:00:12.4 or similar)
  for VF in 0002:00:12.0 0002:00:12.4; do
    echo $VF > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/fsl_enetc_vf/unbind 2>/dev/null
    echo uio_pci_generic > /sys/bus/pci/devices/$VF/driver_override
    echo $VF > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/uio_pci_generic/bind
  done

  pktgen \
    -l 0,2,3 \
    -n 4 \
    -a 0002:00:12.0 \
    -a 0002:00:12.4 \
    -- \
    -T \
    -P \
    -m "2.0, 3.1"

  -m "2.0, 3.1":
    lcore 2 -> port 0, queue 0
    lcore 3 -> port 1, queue 0

7.4 Using LD_LIBRARY_PATH for shared DPDK build only
  export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/lib/dpdk:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH
  pktgen -l 0,2 -n 4 -a 0002:00:12.0 -- -T -P -m "2.0"


Step 8 - pktgen Interactive CLI
================================
Once pktgen starts an interactive terminal is presented.  Example commands:

  # Start / stop traffic on port 0
  start 0
  stop  0
  start all
  stop  all

  # Configure packet parameters
  set 0 size 64
  set 0 rate 100
  set 0 count 0          # 0 = infinite
  set 0 src ip 10.0.0.1/24
  set 0 dst ip 10.0.0.2
  set 0 src mac 00:11:22:33:44:55
  set 0 dst mac 00:aa:bb:cc:dd:ee
  set 0 proto udp
  set 0 sport 1234
  set 0 dport 5678

  # Statistics
  clr      # clear counters
  str      # start traffic
  stp      # stop traffic

  # Quit
  quit


Step 9 - Example Lua Script
=============================
Save as /root/pktgen_test.lua and pass with -f flag:

  package.path = package.path ..";?.lua;test/?.lua;app/?.lua;"
  pktgen.clr()
  pktgen.set("0", "size",  64)
  pktgen.set("0", "rate",  100)
  pktgen.set("0", "count", 0)
  pktgen.set_ipaddr("0", "dst", "10.0.0.2")
  pktgen.set_ipaddr("0", "src", "10.0.0.1/24")
  pktgen.set_mac("0",  "dst", "00:aa:bb:cc:dd:ee")
  pktgen.set("0", "proto", "udp")
  pktgen.set("0", "sport", 1234)
  pktgen.set("0", "dport", 5678)
  pktgen.delay(1000)
  pktgen.start("0")
  pktgen.delay(10000)
  pktgen.stop("0")
  pktgen.delay(500)
  local stats = pktgen.portStats("all", "port")
  for i, s in pairs(stats) do
      print(string.format("Port %d: TX=%d RX=%d", i, s["opackets"], s["ipackets"]))
  end

Run:
  pktgen -l 0,2 -n 4 -a 0002:00:12.0 -- -T -P -m "2.0" -f /root/pktgen_test.lua


Troubleshooting
================

"EAL: No free hugepages"
  echo 512 > /sys/kernel/mm/hugepages/hugepages-2048kB/nr_hugepages

"EAL: No probed ethernet devices" / device not found
  Verify uio_pci_generic is loaded and the VF is bound:
    ls /sys/bus/pci/drivers/uio_pci_generic/   # must list 0002:00:12.0
    ls /dev/uio*                                # must show /dev/uio0 etc.

"cannot open shared object file: librte_*.so" (shared build only)
  echo /usr/lib/dpdk > /etc/ld.so.conf.d/dpdk.conf && ldconfig
  # or: export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/lib/dpdk:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH

"cannot open shared object file: libpcap.so.0.8"
  The runtime libpcap is missing.  Copy it from the sysroot:
    scp ${SYSROOT}/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libpcap.so.1.* root@<board>:/usr/lib/
    ssh root@<board> "cd /usr/lib && ln -sf libpcap.so.1.* libpcap.so.0.8 && ldconfig"

uio_pci_generic bind fails - "write error: No such device"
  The VF may still be attached to its kernel driver.  Unbind first:
    echo 0002:00:12.0 > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/fsl_enetc_vf/unbind

"PMD: enetc4_vf: no descriptor rings"
  The PF must be probed (network interface up) and VFs created before
  binding the VF to uio_pci_generic.

Port shows 0 RX packets in back-to-back test
  Set the destination MAC to the actual MAC of the peer port:
    set 0 dst mac <mac-of-port1>

Low throughput
  - Increase hugepages to 1 GB (hugepages=512 of 2MB).
  - Isolate pktgen lcores from OS scheduler (isolcpus= kernel cmdline).
  - Assign multiple lcores per port: -m "[2:3].0"
  - Try larger burst size: set 0 burst 64


